Maya (CS): Ravi reported onboarding flakiness — a spike of 429s during the first minute after deploy. Logs below.
Ravi (Client): Confirmed. Web app opens 4 concurrent calls on load. Each tab fetches its own ephemeral token (20s TTL).
Dev (SE): 20s TTL + concurrent preflight = stampede. Can you share init snippet?
Ravi (Client): Simplified init:
```js
async function boot(){
 const t = await fetch('/token').then(r=>r.text());
 await Promise.all([api.call(t), api.call(t), api.call(t), api.call(t)]);
}
```
Maya (CS): That pattern prevents coalescing. Gateway treats very short TTL keys as high-fanout.
Polly (SRE): Our policy flags TTL <30s and applies stricter per-key caps to protect shared pools — explains the 429s.
Dev (SE): Short fixes: 1) avoid Promise.all at init; 2) implement singleflight token fetch per origin; 3) cache token locally until expiry-buffer; 4) jittered backoff on 429. Singleflight example: 
```js
let pending=null;
function fetchToken(){
 if(pending) return pending;
 pending = fetch('/token').then(r=>r.text()).finally(()=> pending=null);
 return pending;
}
```
Ravi (Client): Security team worried about longer TTLs. Is session token (~10m) advisable?
Polly (SRE): Yes for onboarding/dev we can allow ~10m session tokens. For strict security, use refresh flow but avoid per-request minting.
Maya (CS): Want a temporary quota lift while you roll client changes?
Ravi (Client): Please. That will buy time to deploy to canaries.
Polly (SRE): Granted temporary bump.
QuotaBot: change applied customer=Sparrow key=web-abc old=100/min new=800/min expires=36h
Ravi (Client): Deployed singleflight+cache to staging — 429s dropped from ~14% to ~1.3%. Cold start still has a few 429s but much better.
Dev (SE): Add client clock-skew check and log token exp so we can monitor reuse window. Also consider a small client-side token leaky-bucket to smooth bursts.
Maya (CS): I'll schedule a 20min enablement call to review SDK best practices and token TTL tradeoffs.
Ravi (Client): thanks all 🙏 Will monitor and report back.
Polly (SRE): Note added to customer record about temporary bump; recommend revert after verification.
Maya (CS): marking resolved for now — reopen if 429 rate >2% after rollback.
